const title       = 'What Is the Wingspan of a Raven?';
const description = 'The Common Raven has a wingspan of 115–150 cm (45–59 inches) — making it the largest passerine bird in the world. This page covers raven wingspan measurements, variation across species, and how raven wingspans compare to other large birds.';
const path        = '/questions/what-is-the-wingspan-of-a-raven/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'What Is the Wingspan of a Raven?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How big is a raven\'s wingspan?', a: 'The Common Raven (Corvus corax) has a wingspan of approximately 115–150 cm (45–59 inches), with the average around 120–130 cm in most populations. This makes it the largest passerine bird (songbird / perching bird) in the world by both wingspan and body mass. Males are slightly larger than females on average, but there is substantial overlap in measurements.' },
  { q: 'Is a raven wingspan bigger than a hawk\'s?', a: 'The Common Raven\'s wingspan (115–150 cm) overlaps with or exceeds that of many common hawks. For comparison: the Red-tailed Hawk (Buteo jamaicensis) spans 110–141 cm; the Buzzard (Buteo buteo) spans 109–136 cm; the Peregrine Falcon spans 89–113 cm. So the largest ravens have wingspans comparable to or exceeding these medium-large raptors. The raven is surpassed in wingspan by eagles and large vultures — the Golden Eagle spans 180–234 cm, and the White-tailed Eagle up to 265 cm.' },
  { q: 'How does raven wingspan compare across species?', a: 'The Common Raven is the largest raven species in wingspan. Chihuahuan Raven wingspan is approximately 100–115 cm. Australian Raven wingspan is approximately 100–110 cm. Little Raven is approximately 85–100 cm. Fan-tailed and Brown-necked Ravens are roughly 100–115 cm. White-necked and Thick-billed Ravens (African highlands species) are 90–105 cm. The Common Raven\'s large wingspan reflects its broad-climate adaptability — a larger wingspan relative to body mass provides better soaring efficiency at high altitudes and in cold, thermally active environments.' },
];
