    <h1>What Is the Wingspan of a Raven?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven has a wingspan of 115–150 cm (45–59 inches), averaging approximately 120–130 cm in most populations. It is the world's largest passerine bird. Wingspan varies with sex (males slightly larger), subspecies, and geographic population. Other raven species are smaller, ranging from approximately 85–115 cm.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Common Raven Wingspan</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Wingspan measurements from museum specimens and field-caught birds across the range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Range:</strong> 115–150 cm (most sources; extremes recorded at ~100 cm for small females of southern races and ~160 cm for large northern males).</li>
      <li><strong>Average:</strong> approximately 120–130 cm across the full range.</li>
      <li><strong>Sexual dimorphism:</strong> males average approximately 5–8% larger than females in linear measurements, translating to a noticeable but not dramatic difference in wingspan.</li>
      <li><strong>Geographic variation:</strong> northern and high-altitude populations (Alaska, Siberia, Himalayas) tend to be larger than southern populations (Canary Islands, Middle East), consistent with Bergmann's Rule (larger body size in colder climates).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Wing Shape and Flight</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven wings are long, broad, and deeply fingered — with conspicuous primary projection (long outermost primaries that spread into fingers in soaring flight). This wing shape is adapted for:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sustained soaring:</strong> ravens exploit thermals and ridge lift extensively; their high aspect ratio and large wing area provide efficient soaring at low airspeeds, allowing them to cover vast distances while expending minimal energy.</li>
      <li><strong>Maneuverability:</strong> despite their large size, ravens are agile in flight — capable of aerial rolls, half-rolls, and diving displays. This maneuverability is achieved partly by asymmetric wing folding and tail fanning.</li>
      <li><strong>Flap-glide:</strong> in level flight over open country, ravens use a distinctive flap-glide pattern — several deep wing beats followed by a glide — which is more energy-efficient than continuous flapping.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Wingspan Comparison</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> How the Common Raven compares to other familiar birds:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> 115–150 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Red-tailed Hawk:</strong> 110–141 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Common Buzzard:</strong> 109–136 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion Crow:</strong> 84–100 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Rook:</strong> 81–99 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Peregrine Falcon:</strong> 89–113 cm</li>
      <li><strong>Golden Eagle:</strong> 180–234 cm (much larger)</li>
      <li><strong>White-tailed Eagle:</strong> 190–265 cm (much larger)</li>
      <li><strong>Eurasian Jay:</strong> 52–58 cm (much smaller)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

